# ts volatility

`volatility` fits models in which the variance of a return series changes with time, in clusters of calm and turbulence. A model `y_t = mean + e_t`, `e_t = sqrt(h_t) z_t` lets the conditional variance `h_t` depend on past shocks and past variances: `Garch` and `Garch11` (symmetric), `Gjr` (larger response to negative shocks) and `Egarch` (the logarithm of the variance, so no positivity constraints). `MvGarch`, `MvGjr` and `MvEgarch` fit several series with a constant conditional correlation.

All fits maximise the Gaussian likelihood. The pre-sample variance that starts the recursion is the mean squared residual, so the likelihood does not depend on the parameters being searched through its start-up value. The mean is the sample mean (when `demean` is on) and is held fixed during the search.

## The Surface

| Entity | What it gives you |
|---|---|
| [`Egarch`](volatility/Egarch.md) | Fits a EGARCH(p, q) model by maximum likelihood |
| [`EgarchFit`](volatility/EgarchFit.md) | The result of a `Egarch` fit: the estimated parameters and the methods that evaluate the fitted model |
| [`EgarchOptions`](volatility/EgarchOptions.md) | Options of `ts::Egarch::fit_mle` |
| [`Garch`](volatility/Garch.md) | Fits a GARCH(p, q) model by maximum likelihood |
| [`Garch11`](volatility/Garch11.md) | Fits a GARCH(1, 1) model by maximum likelihood |
| [`Garch11Fit`](volatility/Garch11Fit.md) | The result of a `Garch11` fit: the estimated parameters and the methods that evaluate the fitted model |
| [`Garch11Options`](volatility/Garch11Options.md) | Options of `ts::Garch11::fit_mle` |
| [`GarchFit`](volatility/GarchFit.md) | The result of a `Garch` fit: the estimated parameters and the methods that evaluate the fitted model |
| [`GarchOptions`](volatility/GarchOptions.md) | Options of `ts::Garch::fit_mle` |
| [`Gjr`](volatility/Gjr.md) | Fits a GJR-GARCH(p, q) model by maximum likelihood |
| [`GjrFit`](volatility/GjrFit.md) | The result of a `Gjr` fit: the estimated parameters and the methods that evaluate the fitted model |
| [`GjrOptions`](volatility/GjrOptions.md) | Options of `ts::Gjr::fit_mle` |
| [`MvEgarch`](volatility/MvEgarch.md) | Fits a constant-conditional-correlation model with a EGARCH variance for every series |
| [`MvEgarchFit`](volatility/MvEgarchFit.md) | The result of a `MvEgarch` fit: one univariate fit per series, the means and the correlation matrix |
| [`MvEgarchOptions`](volatility/MvEgarchOptions.md) | Options of `ts::MvEgarch::fit_mle` |
| [`MvGarch`](volatility/MvGarch.md) | Fits a constant-conditional-correlation model with a GARCH variance for every series |
| [`MvGarchFit`](volatility/MvGarchFit.md) | The result of a `MvGarch` fit: one univariate fit per series, the means and the correlation matrix |
| [`MvGarchOptions`](volatility/MvGarchOptions.md) | Options of `ts::MvGarch::fit_mle` |
| [`MvGjr`](volatility/MvGjr.md) | Fits a constant-conditional-correlation model with a GJR-GARCH variance for every series |
| [`MvGjrFit`](volatility/MvGjrFit.md) | The result of a `MvGjr` fit: one univariate fit per series, the means and the correlation matrix |
| [`MvGjrOptions`](volatility/MvGjrOptions.md) | Options of `ts::MvGjr::fit_mle` |

## How To Call It

Fit a GARCH(1,1) to a series with volatility clustering and read the persistence, the fitted variances and a forecast.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>

#include <cmath>
#include <iomanip>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

namespace ts = pluss::subsp::stats::ts;
using pluss::Series;

const auto f64 = pluss::Config{}.with_dtype(pluss::ScalarType::Float64);
void show(const char* label, const Series& s) {
  std::cout << std::left << std::setw(14) << label << std::right << std::fixed << std::setprecision(4);
  for (double v : s.to_vec<double>()) std::cout << std::setw(9) << v;
  std::cout << "\n";
}
// Returns whose variance responds more to negative shocks: h = 0.05 + (0.05 + 0.20 * 1[r < 0]) r_prev^2 + 0.75 h_prev.
Series returns(int n) {
  std::vector<double> r(n);
  double h = 0.5, prev = 0.0;
  for (int t = 0; t < n; ++t) {
    double u = std::sin(12.9898 * (t + 1.0) + 78.233) * 43758.5453;
    u -= std::floor(u);
    const double z = (u - 0.5) * std::sqrt(12.0);
    h = 0.05 + (0.05 + (prev < 0 ? 0.20 : 0.0)) * prev * prev + 0.75 * h;
    r[t] = std::sqrt(h) * z;
    prev = r[t];
  }
  return pluss::make_series(r, {n}, f64);
}

int main() {
  const Series y = returns(1000);
  const auto fit = ts::Garch11::fit_mle(y);
  std::cout << std::fixed << std::setprecision(4) << "omega " << fit.omega << ", alpha " << fit.alpha << ", beta " << fit.beta << ", persistence " << fit.alpha + fit.beta << "\n";
  show("forecast", fit.forecast(5, y));
}
```

